[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Entity Graphs (Fetch Graphs and Load Graphs) in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Entity Graphs in JPA provide a flexible and declarative way to define the fetching strategy for your queries at runtime. They help solve the classic N+1 select problem and avoid unwanted LazyInitializationException errors without resorting to overly aggressive eager fetching or writing complex custom JOIN FETCH queries."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Fetch Graphs vs. Load Graphs"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"JPA defines two types of Entity Graphs, which differ primarily in how they treat unmentioned attributes:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fetch Graphs (GraphSemantic.FETCH): Attributes explicitly specified in the graph are treated as EAGER. All other attributes not specified are treated as LAZY, regardless of their default mapping annotations."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Load Graphs (GraphSemantic.LOAD): Attributes explicitly specified in the graph are treated as EAGER. All other attributes not specified retain their default fetch type defined in the entity annotations."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining an Entity Graph"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define an entity graph statically using the @NamedEntityGraph annotation directly on your entity class. Below is an example of an Order entity that includes a customer and order items."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@NamedEntityGraph(
    name = "Order.detail",
    attributeNodes = {
        @NamedAttributeNode("customer"),
        @NamedAttributeNode(value = "items", subgraph = "item-subgraph")
    },
    subgraphs = {
        @NamedSubgraph(
            name = "item-subgraph",
            attributeNodes = {
                @NamedAttributeNode("product")
            }
        )
    }
)
public class Order {
    @Id
    private Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private Customer customer;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying Entity Graphs in Queries"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once defined, you can pass the entity graph to the EntityManager using query hints. The hint name depends on whether you are using a fetch graph or a load graph."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"EntityGraph<?> graph = entityManager.getEntityGraph("Order.detail");

Map<String, Object> hints = new HashMap<>();
hints.put("javax.persistence.fetchgraph", graph); // Use "jakarta.persistence.fetchgraph" for Jakarta EE 9+

Order order = entityManager.find(Order.class, 1L, hints);"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Entity Graphs with TypedQuery"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Entity graphs can also be applied to JPQL queries executed via TypedQuery by setting the hint directly on the query object."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"EntityGraph<?> graph = entityManager.getEntityGraph("Order.detail");

TypedQuery<Order> query = entityManager.createQuery("SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.id = :id", Order.class);
query.setParameter("id", 1L);
query.setHint("javax.persistence.loadgraph", graph);

Order order = query.getSingleResult();"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Fetch Graphs when you want strict control over exactly what gets loaded, ensuring that unmentioned large relationships do not accidentally trigger database hits."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Load Graphs when you want to augment the default fetching strategy (e.g., adding an extra eager join for a specific use case) without overriding the rest of the entity mapping."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your subgraphs modular and reuse them across similar queries to prevent code duplication."}]
